+    "status": "planned follow-up; runs only after the main seven-arm comparison",
+    "question": "Does the best schedule depend on the healing phase (early, mid, late)?",
+    "why_branching": "Independent arms spread their plan over the whole budget, so what an arm does in a phase depends on its budget, and its speed in a phase mixes schedule quality with the state the schedule left behind. Branching from one shared checkpoint removes both confounds.",
+    "design": [
+      "One trunk run saves checkpoints at the first evaluation closing 25%, 50% and 75% of the validation gap (--checkpoint-gaps).",
+      "From each checkpoint, all seven schedules continue with equal budgets and fresh schedules (--init-from); a checkpoint carries weights, Adam state, per-group update counts and the data position.",
+      "Score each branch on the same ladder as the main arms (thresholds from the trunk's damaged start): FLOPs and tokens to the next rung, and gap closed at the end of its budget.",
+      "Then race a composite (best early -> best mid -> best late) against the best single schedule run end to end, over several seeds."
+    ],
+    "open_choices": [
+      "trunk schedule (proposed: the main study's best single schedule)",
+      "branch budget per phase",
+      "seeds per branch point"
+    ],
+    "tested": "test_branch_resume_matches_uninterrupted_run: trunk checkpoint + branch equals an uninterrupted run bit for bit on CPU"
   }
